{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011CeAXFsgZiXR2maS9Lm7Nt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Given the huge number of files to update, I think the fastest and most reliable approach is to write a comprehensive bash script that uses sed to update all `new APIError(` call sites across the monorepo. However, sed is tricky for multi-line patterns. Let me think of a better approach.\n\nActually, let me use a node script that does a reliable find-and-replace on all files. This will be more precise than sed for handling multi-line patterns.\n\nWait, actually, looking at the patterns, most `new APIError(` calls follow a few patterns:\n\n1. `new APIError('message string', statusCode)` - single line\n2. with extra data/isPublic args, 3. multi-line versions, and 4. template literal versions. For each, I need to insert an appropriate error code as the first argument, which means I'll need a script that scans each call site, infers a sensible code from the message/context, and rewrites the call accordingly given the ~150+ occurrences involved.\n\nI'm settling on mapping error codes primarily by HTTP status (400→BAD_REQUEST, 401→UNAUTHORIZED, 403→FORBIDDEN, 404→NOT_FOUND, 413→PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE, 422→UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY, 423→LOCKED, 500→INTERNAL_ERROR), with more specific codes derived from message text like \"Missing ID\" or \"Invalid\" when it's clearer. I'll write a Node.js script to apply this mapping across all the files since handling multi-line patterns manually would be too error-prone.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1052,"cache_read_input_tokens":66583,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1052},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4da9476c-44c5-4fb2-a